EXPECTED: NO_DOS_EFFECT

REASON: Netty 4.1.136.Final adds writeSucceeded guard that releases ByteBuf in catch block — assumption A1 is refuted

TARGET_IMAGE: eclipse-temurin:21-jre-jammy (custom Netty HTTP/2 server, netty-codec-http2:4.1.136.Final)
TARGET_PORT: 8443/tcp
CLIENT_TOOL: h2

DISPROOF_METHOD: assumption_refuted
DISPROOF_DETAIL: |
  Opened an HTTP/2 connection with gzip content-encoding, completed a stream to trigger
  decompressor cleanup, then sent DATA frames for the cleaned-up stream against Netty
  4.1.136.Final. Each writeInbound ClosedChannelException was caught with writeSucceeded
  == false, so data.release() was called correctly. JVM direct memory stayed flat
  throughout a 60s sustained attack. No OutOfMemoryError observed. Static analysis of
  DelegatingDecompressorFrameListener in 4.1.136.Final confirms the writeSucceeded flag
  and the release call in the catch block. Fix introduced July 2026 (CVE-2026-56819).
DISPROOF_NOTES: Assumption A1 ("ByteBuf not released on ClosedChannelException") is
                refuted. Netty 4.1.136.Final introduced the writeSucceeded flag in
                Http2Decompressor.decompress(). The concurrency-pivot attack (racing
                stream resets with DATA frames) is also blocked: the guard is per-call,
                not per-connection.
